      "text": "Given that a nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients has all of the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\;3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\;1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we first note that if a polynomial with rational coefficients has a root that is an irrational number, then its conjugate must also be a root. Therefore, the conjugates of the given roots must also be roots of the polynomial.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are:\n\\[1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 1-\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 2-\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; 3-\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}, \\; 1000-\\sqrt{1001}.\\]\n\nThis means the polynomial must have the roots:\n\\[1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 1-\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 2-\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; 3-\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}, \\; 1000-\\sqrt{1001}.\\]\n\nThe polynomial must be of the form whose roots are these conjugate pairs. Since there are 1000 pairs of conjugate roots, the total degree of the polynomial is \\(1000 \\times 2 = 2000\\).\n\nThus, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).",
