      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let's denote the polynomial with rational coefficients that has the roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\). Since the polynomial has rational coefficients, the conjugates of these roots must also be roots. Therefore, the complete set of roots includes \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, 2-\\sqrt{3}, 3-\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\).\n\nThe polynomial can be expressed as the product of the minimal polynomials of these roots. The minimal polynomial of a root \\(k + \\sqrt{m}\\) is \\((x - (k + \\sqrt{m}))(x - (k - \\sqrt{m})) = (x - k - \\sqrt{m})(x - k + \\sqrt{m}) = (x - k)^2 - m\\). Applying this to each root, we get the minimal polynomial for each root \\(k + \\sqrt{m}\\) as \\((x - (k + \\sqrt{m}))(x - (k - \\sqrt{m})) = x^2 - (2k)x + (k^2 - m)\\).\n\nThe polynomial with the roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) is:\n\\[\n(x - (1+\\sqrt{2}))(x - (1-\\sqrt{2}))(x - (2+\\sqrt{3}))(x - (2-\\sqrt{3})) \\cdots (x - (1000+\\sqrt{1001}))(x - (1000-\\sqrt{1001}))\n\\]\nSimplifying the first set of conjugates, we get:\n\\[\n(x - 1 - \\sqrt{2})(x - 1 + \\sqrt{2}) = (x - 1)^2 - 2 = x^2 - 2x + 1 - 2 = x^2 - 2x - 1\n\\]\nSimplifying the second set of conjugates, we get:\n\\[\n(x - 2 - \\sqrt{3})(x - 2 + \\sqrt{3}) = (x - 2)^2 - 3 = x^2 - 4x + 4 - 3 = x^2 - 4x + 1\n\\]\nContinuing this pattern, the minimal polynomial for the set \\(k + \\sqrt{m}\\) is \\(x^2 - 2kx + (k^2 - m)\\). Therefore, the polynomial with all the given roots is:\n\\[\n(x^2 - 2 \\cdot 1 x - 1)(x^2 - 2 \\cdot 2 x - 1) \\cdots (x^2 - 2 \\cdot 1000 x - 1) (x^2 - 4 x + 1) \\cdots (x^2 - 1000 x + 1000)\n\\]\nThere are 1000 minimal polynomials of the form \\(x^2 - 2kx + (k^2 - m)\\), and the polynomial of the form \\(x^2 - 4x + 1\\) appears 1000 times. Therefore, the total degree of the polynomial is:\n\\[\n1000 \\cdot 2 + 1000 = 2000 + 1000 = 3000\n\\]\nThus, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is \\(\\boxed{3000}\\).",
